    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm seeing some weird damage on my tomato plants in the greenhouse. there are these tiny little holes in the leaves, like they've been nibbled on, and it's happening in a few different spots across the batch. i can't see any obvious bugs during the day. any idea what might be causing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those tiny holes in the leaves can be quite a mystery, but I'm sure we can dig up some clues. It's possible that your tomato plants are under attack by some sneaky, nocturnal pests. You know, like a goblin's favorite snack - a midnight snack of juicy tomato leaves. \n\nIn all seriousness, though, there are a few possibilities. One common culprit is the tomato hornworm, which can cause similar damage. However, since you can't see any bugs during the day, it's possible that the hornworms are hiding in the plants' foliage or are nocturnal feeders. \n\nAnother possibility is that your plants are being attacked by a type of fungus, like a leaf spot or a fungal disease. But, just like a goblin's lair, these diseases can be tricky to spot, especially if they're not causing any visible symptoms.\n\nLastly, it's also possible that the damage is caused by a type of insect that's not typically active during the day, like a spider mite or a thrips. These tiny critters can be quite sneaky, and their damage can be difficult to spot.\n\nTo help you get to the bottom of this mystery, I'd recommend taking a closer look",
